<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Grand Council (Qing)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1729</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Grand Council</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Grand Council was a small advisory body established by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yongzheng-emperor">Yongzheng Emperor</a> around 1729. It handled sensitive military and political matters and concentrated decision-making in the emperor's hands, becoming the most important organ of government in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing Empire</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-empire">Italian Empire</a> link reflects the separate <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-council-of-fascism">Grand Council of Fascism</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/grand-council-qing.jpg" alt="Grand Council (Qing)" width="320" height="224" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Gisling · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jun_Ji_Chu.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Great Law created a <b>Grand Council</b> of fifty hereditary chiefs, the hoyaneh, whose titles were distributed unequally among the nations: fourteen for the Onondaga, ten for the Cayuga, nine each for the Mohawk and Oneida, and eight for the Seneca.</blockquote><a href="/empire/iroquois-confederacy">Iroquois Confederacy · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Grand Council</b>, established by the Yongzheng Emperor around 1729 as a small inner body for urgent military and political matters, concentrated decision-making close to the emperor and bypassed the more cumbersome Grand Secretariat, making government more responsive during the dynasty’s vigorous middle period.</blockquote><a href="/empire/qing-empire">Qing Empire · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>All important matters were decided in the Inner Court, dominated by the imperial family and the Manchu nobility, where the Yongzheng Emperor created the <b>Grand Council</b> in the 1720s.</blockquote><a href="/societies/qing-china">Social Hierarchy of Qing China</a></li><li><blockquote>The Cayuga sent ten chiefs to the <b>Grand Council</b>, where they sat with the Oneida as the younger brothers.</blockquote><a href="/state/cayuga-nation">Cayuga Nation</a></li><li><blockquote>Nine Mohawk chiefs sat in the <b>Grand Council</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/mohawk-nation">Mohawk Nation</a></li><li><blockquote>Like the other member nations, the Oneida were organised in clans, with women owning the longhouses and fields and clan mothers selecting the chiefs who represented the nation, nine of them, at the <b>Grand Council</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/oneida-nation">Oneida Nation</a></li></ul></section>
